#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Per-demo NARRATIVE derivation check (K-1051).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `kiosk-demo-*/before-after.md` is a PUBLISHED per-demo
# surface — four documents, ~1780 lines, quoting recorded transcripts and
# shipped source at length — and until this script nothing compared a single
# byte of it against anything. bin/check-demo-copies:SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED names
# the file only to EXCLUDE it («per-demo narrative: that storefront's
# before/after contrast»), and unlike the README entry beside it, that exclusion
# carried no pointer to a checker of its own. bin/check-ci-tasks and
# audit/check-file-inventories.rb never mention it.
#
# The cost was measured, not assumed. Ten ledger rows live in these four files
# (K-1053..K-1057, K-1062..K-1066, and K-774/K-778/K-812/K-817/K-818/K-832/K-1036
# before them) and EVERY ONE was found by a human eye reading the documents in
# one sitting — never by a gate. K-1062 is the shape that matters: getgrocery
# and hoteling published transcript blocks under an explicit «verbatim … nothing
# else touched» guarantee that contained five and three assertion strings the
# tasks no longer print, omitted getgrocery's trailing `Server stopped.`, and in
# hoteling's case omitted an entire RUN the task prints.
#
# WHY IT IS A SEPARATE SCRIPT, AND NOT A SECTION OF bin/check-demo-copies
# (K-1096). That file's own header states the boundary it holds: bin/check-ci-tasks
# is a SIBLING rather than a mode of it «because the two answer different
# questions from different inputs: that one compares ci.yml against each demo's
# rake tasks, this one compares demo files against each other». The boundary is
# the comparison INPUT. This check's input is a THIRD pair — one demo's document
# against THAT SAME DEMO's own producers — so it is a third sibling by the very
# rule that kept the first two apart. (The cross-demo half of the narrative
# class, where the input IS demo-against-demo, is correctly in check-demo-copies
# already: the K-1069 framework-prose block.)
#
# ── THE THREE DESIGN FACTS THIS IS BUILT ON, each bought by a repair ──────────
#
# 1. A BLOCK MUST NAME ITS PRODUCER MACHINE-READABLY. The four documents named
#    theirs in prose, four different ways; prose parsing rots. So every fenced
#    block carries an HTML comment on the line immediately above it:
#
#      <!-- derived: transcript | task: bundle exec rake demo:book
#           | from: lib/tasks/demo.rake, script/book_flow.rb | abridged: none -->
#      <!-- derived: snippet | from: app/controllers/kiosk/x_controller.rb
#           | transform: strip_descriptions | abridged: two marked elisions -->
#      <!-- derived: none | why: the generator invocation an adopter types -->
#
#    A fenced block with no declaration FAILS. That is the property that matters
#    most: a new block cannot appear silently, the same way a new duplicated path
#    cannot in bin/check-demo-copies.
#
# 2. TWO BLOCK CLASSES, TWO CHECKS — one check cannot serve both.
#    TRANSCRIPT: «every line is a line the named task prints».
#    SNIPPET:    «every line is a line of that file, modulo the declared
#                 transformation».
#
# 3. LINE MEMBERSHIP, NOT EQUALITY, AND NEVER REGENERATION. UUIDs, settlement
#    ids, `pi_…` references, ports and dates change every run, so a guard cannot
#    diff a block against a fresh run — and worse, getgrocery's transcript has
#    two legitimate shapes depending on the Dublin hour (the K-480 past-slot
#    control is a no-op after the last slot of the day), so a guard that
#    REGENERATES flaps where one checking membership does not. This check never
#    boots Rails, never touches a database and never runs a demo.
#
# ── HOW THE TRANSCRIPT CHECK WORKS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ripper lexes each declared producer and every string literal in it becomes a
# LINE PATTERN: literal text with `#{…}` interpolations replaced by a wildcard,
# split on newlines, adjacent `"a" \ "b"` literals merged (that shape is how
# most long assertion messages are written, and treating the halves as two
# patterns would make the check useless on exactly the lines K-1062 was about).
#
# A transcript line is ACCOUNTED FOR when one of these holds:
#
#   (a) a pattern matches it whole;
#   (b) a pattern matches it whole as a FRAME and every wildcard it captured is
#       itself accounted for — empty, a single whitespace-free token (an id, a
#       number, a URL, a path), or a pattern in its own right. This is what
#       resolves `puts "  OK    #{msg}"` against the `msg` literal two hundred
#       lines away, without the frame's `.*?` swallowing arbitrary prose;
#   (c) it is a JSON object — the driver's summary line — and every key at every
#       depth is a name some producer names. Its NESTED keys are the handler's,
#       not the driver's (the driver splices a response object in whole), so a
#       transcript may name the handler in `keys_from:`. That is a SEPARATE list
#       on purpose: those files are consulted for key names only and their
#       literals never join the line-pattern set, because a controller is large
#       and folding it in would blunt the line check this exists for.
#
# Anything else is an error quoting the line. A doc line carrying a message the
# task no longer prints is exactly case (b) failing, which is all five stale
# getgrocery strings and all three hoteling ones.
#
# ── HOW THE SNIPPET CHECK WORKS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every line must be a member of the UNION of the source's raw lines and the
# lines of each DECLARED transformation of that source. The transformations are
# EXECUTABLE, not prose:
#
#   strip_descriptions — deletes each verb's prose `description` call (leaving
#                        the marker line the documents already use) and every
#                        `description:` key inside a schema, reattaching what
#                        followed it. This is the transformation the four
#                        documents describe in prose; here it is a function.
#   dedent             — the source's own indentation is not required to
#                        survive quoting (config/routes.rb is quoted without the
#                        `routes.draw do` indent). Compares lstripped.
#
# The document's OWN comments inside a snippet are permitted only through the
# marked-elision shape the repair wave introduced:
#
#   # ── <WHAT> ELIDED HERE (this comment is the document's, not the file's) ──
#
# followed by a run of comment lines. A document comment that is not introduced
# by that exact marker is an error, because it is indistinguishable from a line
# the reader will take as shipped code.
#
# ── AND THE PROSE-PROMISE RULE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ABRIDGEMENT MUST BE DECLARED IN A FORM THIS SCRIPT READS, NOT PROMISED IN
# PROSE. Every «nothing else touched» / «nothing removed and nothing added» /
# «unedited» / «verbatim» promise in these four documents was FALSE when K-1062
# and K-1063 read them — so the promise is exactly the sentence that must be
# BACKED by something mechanical rather than trusted. Three rules, and note what
# they deliberately are NOT:
#
#   * A promise in the paragraph immediately before or after a block declared
#     `derived: none` is an ERROR. That block is checked by nothing, so dressing
#     it as verbatim is the one case where the prose is the ONLY guarantee.
#   * `abridged: none` and a `transform:`, or `abridged: none` and a marked
#     elision inside the block, are a CONTRADICTION between the declaration and
#     the block, and fail.
#   * A declared `transform:` that no line in the block needs is DECORATION and
#     fails — a transformation is declared because it was applied, and one
#     nobody applied is a promise again.
#
#   They are NOT a ban on the phrases. A qualified promise — «every line is that
#   file's with its `description` cut out and nothing else altered» — is what the
#   K-1062 repair wave deliberately wrote, and it is TRUE precisely because
#   `strip_descriptions` below makes it checkable. Banning the sentence would
#   delete the honest form along with the dishonest one; what this script does
#   instead is refuse to let the sentence stand alone.
#
# ── WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** COVER. Stated plainly, because an over-claimed guard
# is the defect this class keeps producing and K-1069's block was accepted
# precisely for declaring its own holes. ────────────────────────────────────
#
#   * IT CANNOT PROVE A TRANSCRIPT IS COMPLETE. Membership is a SUBSET test. A
#     block that silently drops an entire RUN the task prints — which is half of
#     K-1062 — still passes. Completeness needs a real run, which is what
#     `abridged:` declares instead of proves, and `abridged: none` is therefore
#     a HUMAN's assertion this script only keeps consistent with the prose.
#   * IT CANNOT PROVE THE LINES ARE IN THE RIGHT ORDER, or that they came from
#     ONE run. Two lines from mutually exclusive branches both pass.
#   * IT CANNOT SEE A LINE PRINTED BY A PRODUCER NOBODY DECLARED. `from:` is a
#     human's list; a line that came from an undeclared file simply fails, and a
#     wrong line that happens to match a declared file's literal simply passes.
#   * A LINE WITH NO LITERAL TEXT AT ALL — `puts x` — is invisible to the
#     transcript check unless it is JSON. Its content is unconstrained.
#   * THE JSON RULE CHECKS KEYS, NEVER VALUES. `"http_pay":200` and
#     `"http_pay":500` are equally acceptable here.
#   * `derived: none` IS AN UNCHECKED ESCAPE HATCH. It demands a `why:` and
#     nothing more, exactly as `:per_demo` does in bin/check-demo-copies.
#   * THE SNIPPET CHECK IS SET MEMBERSHIP, so it cannot see a reordering, a
#     duplication, or a deletion — only a line that is in no version of the file.
#   * IT DOES NOT ADJUDICATE WHETHER A COMPLETENESS PROMISE IS TRUE. Membership
#     backs «every line quoted here is a real line», never «no line is missing»,
#     so a document may still promise more than this script can see — which is
#     why `abridged:` is a DECLARATION a human signs, not a proof.
#   * IT SAYS NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE PROSE. Whether the paragraphs around a block
#     are true is K-1069's question (cross-demo agreement, in check-demo-copies)
#     and claims.md's.
#
# Stdlib only (ripper + json), no bundle, no database, no Rails boot, well under
# a second on a bare checkout — the same profile as bin/check-demo-copies.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-demo-derivations           # check
#   bin/check-demo-derivations --list    # print every declared block

require "json"
require "ripper"
require "set"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
DOC  = "before-after.md"

# The corpus, declared rather than discovered, for the same reason `ships:` is
# declared in bin/check-demo-copies: a demo that grows a narrative document, or
# drops one, changes the set with nothing noticing. prove has none — it is the
# API-only broker, it tells no storefront story.
DOCUMENTED_DEMOS = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling skooti].freeze

# The one shape a document's own comment may take inside a quoted snippet.
ELISION_MARKER = /\A\s*#\s*──\s+.+\s+ELIDED HERE \(this comment is the document's, not the file's\)\s+──\s*\z/

# What `strip_descriptions` leaves where a `description` call was. The four
# documents already use this exact line; it is here because the transformation
# has to be executable, and this is the executable half of it.
ELIDED_DESCRIPTION = 'description "…"   # elided — see the shipped file'

# Completeness promises. These are the sentences K-1062 and K-1063 found false;
# they are not banned outright (see the header — the repair wave's qualified
# forms are true and checkable), but a block that this script does not check may
# not carry one in the paragraph on either side of it.
PROMISES = [
  /nothing\s+else\s+(?:is\s+)?(?:touched|altered|left\s+out|removed|added)/i,
  /nothing\s+(?:is\s+)?removed\s+and\s+nothing\s+(?:is\s+)?added/i,
  /\bunedited\b/i,
  /\bverbatim\b/i,
  /first\s+line\s+to\s+last/i,
  /\bthe\s+\*?\*?whole\*?\*?\s+output\b/i,
].freeze

TRANSFORMS = %w[strip_descriptions dedent].freeze

WILD = "␀WILD␀"

ESCAPES = { "n" => "\n", "t" => "\t", "s" => " ", "r" => "\r", "e" => "\e",
            "0" => "\0", "a" => "\a", "b" => "\b", "f" => "\f", "v" => "\v" }.freeze

def unescape(str)
  str.gsub(/\\(u\{[0-9a-fA-F ]+\}|u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|.)/m) do
    c = Regexp.last_match(1)
    if ESCAPES.key?(c)             then ESCAPES[c]
    elsif c.start_with?("u{")      then c[2..-2].split.map { |h| h.to_i(16) }.pack("U*")
    elsif c.start_with?("u")       then [c[1..].to_i(16)].pack("U")
    else                                c
    end
  end
end

# Every string literal in `path`, as line patterns with `#{…}` replaced by WILD.
#
# ADJACENT LITERALS ARE MERGED. `"a" \` newline `"b"` is one Ruby string and is
# how nearly every long assertion message in these demos is written; Ripper
# reports it as two literals, and treating it as two would leave the check
# unable to see the very lines K-1062 recorded.
def line_patterns(path)
  toks = Ripper.lex(File.read(path))
  out  = []
  buf  = nil
  depth = 0
  gap = false

  flush = lambda do
    next if buf.nil?
    out.concat(buf.split("\n", -1))
    buf = nil
  end

  toks.each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    if depth.positive?
      case type
      when :on_embexpr_beg, :on_lbrace then depth += 1
      when :on_embexpr_end, :on_rbrace then depth -= 1
      end
      next
    end

    case type
    when :on_tstring_beg, :on_heredoc_beg, :on_words_beg, :on_qwords_beg, :on_regexp_beg
      if buf && gap
        gap = false          # adjacent literal: keep accumulating into `buf`
      else
        flush.call
        buf = +""
      end
    when :on_tstring_content
      buf << unescape(tok) if buf
    when :on_embexpr_beg
      if buf
        buf << WILD
        depth = 1
      end
    when :on_tstring_end, :on_heredoc_end, :on_regexp_end
      gap = true if buf
    when :on_sp
      nil                    # spaces and `\`-continued newlines do not break adjacency
    else
      flush.call if gap
      gap = false
    end
  end
  flush.call
  out
end

# Key names a producer NAMES: `foo:` labels, `:foo` symbols and bare-identifier
# string literals. The JSON summary line's keys are checked against this.
def key_names(path)
  names = Set.new
  Ripper.lex(File.read(path)).each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    case type
    when :on_label      then names << tok.chomp(":")
    when :on_ident, :on_const then names << tok
    when :on_tstring_content
      names << tok if tok.match?(/\A[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*\z/i)
    end
  end
  names
end

def anchor_strength(pattern)
  pattern.split(WILD, -1).sum { |part| part.gsub(/\s/, "").length }
end

def pattern_regex(pattern, capture:)
  parts = pattern.split(WILD, -1).map { |p| Regexp.escape(p) }
  glue  = capture ? "(.*?)" : ".*?"
  Regexp.new("\\A#{parts.join(glue)}\\z", Regexp::MULTILINE)
end

# How deep the frame composition may go. `puts "  OK    #{msg}"` with `msg`
# itself built as `"… (got #{x})"` is two levels, and that is the deepest shape
# these four demos use; the limit is what keeps a 500-pattern corpus from
# exploring its own cross product.
MAX_DEPTH = 3

class Patterns
  def initialize(paths, key_paths = [])
    pats   = paths.flat_map { |p| line_patterns(p) }.uniq
    @exact = Set.new(pats.reject { |p| p.include?(WILD) }.map(&:rstrip).reject(&:empty?))
    framed = pats.select { |p| p.include?(WILD) }
    # A frame matched WITHOUT checking its wildcards needs real literal text of
    # its own: `"#{a}#{b}"` matches everything and would make this a rubber
    # stamp. A frame whose wildcards ARE checked can be as thin as `"  OK  "` —
    # the capture has to resolve to a literal, so the `.*?` cannot swallow
    # arbitrary prose. That distinction is the whole reason there are two lists.
    @loose = framed.select { |p| anchor_strength(p) >= 4 }.map { |p| pattern_regex(p, capture: false) }
    @tight = framed.select { |p| anchor_strength(p) >= 1 }.map { |p| pattern_regex(p, capture: true) }
    @keys  = (paths + key_paths).flat_map { |p| key_names(p).to_a }.to_set
    @memo  = {}
  end

  def key?(name) = @keys.include?(name)

  def accounts_for?(line, depth = 0)
    line = line.rstrip
    return true if @exact.include?(line)
    return false if depth >= MAX_DEPTH

    key = [line, depth]
    return @memo[key] if @memo.key?(key)

    @memo[key] = false # cut recursion on a cycle
    ok = @loose.any? { |re| re.match?(line) } ||
         @tight.any? do |re|
           m = re.match(line)
           m && m.captures.all? { |c| capture_ok?(c, depth) }
         end
    @memo[key] = ok
  end

  # A wildcard's captured text stands on its own when it is empty or a single
  # whitespace-free token (an id, a number, a URL, a path). Anything with a
  # space in it is prose, and prose must come from a literal.
  def capture_ok?(text, depth)
    t = text.strip
    return true if t.empty?
    return true if t !~ /\s/

    accounts_for?(t, depth + 1)
  end
end

# ── The declared, EXECUTABLE transformations ─────────────────────────────────

# Index just past the end of a (possibly `\`-continued) string literal starting
# at or after `lines[li][ci]`, plus whatever follows it on that final line.
def scan_string_value(lines, li, ci)
  i = li
  col = ci
  loop do
    s = lines[i] or return [li, ""]
    q = s.index(/["']/, col) or return [i, ""]
    quote = s[q]
    j = q + 1
    j += 1 while j < s.length && !(s[j] == quote && s[j - 1] != "\\")
    tail = s[(j + 1)..] || ""
    return [i, tail] unless tail.rstrip.end_with?("\\")

    i += 1
    col = 0
  end
end

def strip_descriptions(src)
  lines = src.split("\n", -1)
  out = []
  i = 0
  while i < lines.length
    line = lines[i]

    if (m = line.match(/\A(\s*)description\s+["']/))
      last, = scan_string_value(lines, i, m[1].length + "description".length)
      out << "#{m[1]}#{ELIDED_DESCRIPTION}"
      i = last + 1
      next
    end

    if (k = line.index(/(?<![\w:])description:\s/))
      pre = line[0...k]
      last, tail = scan_string_value(lines, i, k + "description:".length)
      tail = tail.sub(/\A\s*,/, ",")
      if pre.strip.empty?
        out << (out.pop || "").rstrip.sub(/,\z/, "") + tail
      else
        out << pre.rstrip.sub(/,\z/, "") + tail
      end
      i = last + 1
      next
    end

    out << line
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

def transformed_lines(src, name)
  case name
  when "strip_descriptions" then strip_descriptions(src)
  when "dedent"             then src.split("\n", -1).map(&:lstrip)
  else raise ArgumentError, name
  end
end

# ── Document parsing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Block = Struct.new(:fence_line, :info, :body, :decl, :decl_line, :neighbourhood, keyword_init: true)

DECL_RE = /\A<!--\s*derived:\s*(.*?)\s*-->\z/

def parse_declaration(text)
  fields = {}
  text.split("|").each_with_index do |part, idx|
    if idx.zero?
      fields["derived"] = part.strip
      next
    end
    k, v = part.split(":", 2)
    next if v.nil?

    fields[k.strip] = v.strip
  end
  fields
end

def parse_document(path)
  lines = File.readlines(path, chomp: true)
  blocks = []
  prose_from = 0
  i = 0
  while i < lines.length
    unless lines[i].start_with?("```")
      i += 1
      next
    end

    info = lines[i][3..].to_s.strip
    j = i + 1
    j += 1 while j < lines.length && !lines[j].start_with?("```")
    decl_line = nil
    decl = nil
    if i.positive? && (m = DECL_RE.match(lines[i - 1].strip))
      decl_line = i - 1
      decl = parse_declaration(m[1])
    end
    blocks << Block.new(
      fence_line: i + 1, info: info, body: lines[(i + 1)...j],
      decl: decl, decl_line: decl_line && decl_line + 1,
      neighbourhood: [paragraph_before(lines, decl_line || i), paragraph_after(lines, j)].join("\n"),
    )
    i = j + 1
  end
  blocks
end

# The ONE paragraph on each side of a block — the prose that introduces it and
# the prose that comments on it. Deliberately not "everything since the previous
# block": the gap between two blocks belongs to both of them, so a wider window
# attributes one document's sentence to a block it was never about.
def paragraph_before(lines, idx)
  k = idx - 1
  k -= 1 while k >= 0 && lines[k].strip.empty?
  stop = k
  k -= 1 while k >= 0 && !lines[k].strip.empty?
  lines[(k + 1)..stop].to_a.join("\n")
end

def paragraph_after(lines, idx)
  k = idx + 1
  k += 1 while k < lines.length && lines[k].strip.empty?
  start = k
  k += 1 while k < lines.length && !lines[k].strip.empty? && !lines[k].start_with?("```")
  lines[start...k].to_a.join("\n")
end

# ── Checks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

errors = []
listing = ARGV.include?("--list")
counts = Hash.new(0)

present = Dir.glob(File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-*", DOC)).map { |p| File.basename(File.dirname(p)).sub("kiosk-demo-", "") }.sort
undeclared = present - DOCUMENTED_DEMOS
missing = DOCUMENTED_DEMOS - present
undeclared.each do |demo|
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC} exists but #{demo} is not in " \
            "DOCUMENTED_DEMOS. A demo that grows a narrative document joins the corpus by being " \
            "declared, never by being discovered — the same reason `ships:` is declared in " \
            "bin/check-demo-copies."
end
missing.each do |demo|
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: #{demo} is in DOCUMENTED_DEMOS but ships no #{DOC}. Delete the " \
            "entry or restore the document; a corpus that silently shrinks checks nothing."
end

(DOCUMENTED_DEMOS & present).each do |demo|
  dir = File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-#{demo}")
  doc = File.join(dir, DOC)
  label = "kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC}"
  rake_files = Dir.glob(File.join(dir, "lib/tasks/*.rake"))

  parse_document(doc).each do |block|
    where = "#{label}:#{block.fence_line}"

    if block.decl.nil?
      errors << "#{where}: fenced block with no `<!-- derived: … -->` declaration on the line above " \
                "it. Every block in a narrative document must name its producer machine-readably " \
                "(`transcript`, `snippet`) or be declared `none` with a reason — a block nobody " \
                "declared is a block nobody checks, which is how ~30 defects reached these four " \
                "documents (K-1051)."
      next
    end

    kind = block.decl["derived"]
    unknown = block.decl.keys - %w[derived task from keys_from transform abridged why]
    unless unknown.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: unknown key(s) #{unknown.join(', ')} in the derivation declaration."
    end

    if kind == "none"
      if block.decl["why"].to_s.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: none` needs a `why:` — an undeclared exemption is the one " \
                  "thing this check cannot see through, so it must at least be argued for."
      end
      if (hit = PROMISES.find { |re| block.neighbourhood =~ re })
        errors << "#{where}: the prose beside this block promises completeness " \
                  "(#{block.neighbourhood[hit].inspect}) while the block is declared " \
                  "`derived: none`, so nothing checks it. A promise no mechanism backs is the " \
                  "shape K-1062 and K-1063 found false eight times over — either derive the " \
                  "block from a producer or drop the promise."
      end
      counts[:none] += 1
      puts "  none       #{where}  (#{block.decl['why']})" if listing
      next
    end

    unless %w[transcript snippet].include?(kind)
      errors << "#{where}: `derived: #{kind}` is not one of transcript, snippet, none."
      next
    end

    froms = block.decl["from"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?)
    if froms.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `derived: #{kind}` needs a `from:` naming the producer file(s), " \
                "demo-relative."
      next
    end
    keys_from = block.decl["keys_from"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?)
    if !keys_from.empty? && kind != "transcript"
      errors << "#{where}: `keys_from:` is a transcript-only declaration — it names the handlers " \
                "whose field names appear in the driver's JSON summary line."
      next
    end
    absent = (froms + keys_from).reject { |f| File.file?(File.join(dir, f)) }
    unless absent.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `from:`/`keys_from:` names #{absent.join(', ')}, which kiosk-demo-#{demo} " \
                "does not ship. A producer that moved leaves the block quoting nothing."
      next
    end

    abridged = block.decl["abridged"].to_s
    if abridged.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `abridged:` is required — `none`, or a phrase naming what was left out. " \
                "Abridgement must be DECLARED in a form this check reads; every prose promise of " \
                "the «nothing else touched» kind in these four documents was false (K-1062/K-1063)."
      next
    end

    if kind == "transcript"
      task = block.decl["task"].to_s
      if task.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: transcript` needs a `task:` naming the command that printed it."
        next
      end
      name = task.split.last.to_s
      short = name.split(":").last
      defined_here = rake_files.any? do |rf|
        body = File.read(rf)
        body.match?(/^\s*task\s+:#{Regexp.escape(short)}\b/) ||
          body.match?(/^\s*task\s+#{Regexp.escape(short)}:/)
      end
      unless defined_here
        errors << "#{where}: `task: #{task}` names `#{name}`, which no lib/tasks/*.rake in " \
                  "kiosk-demo-#{demo} defines. The block claims a producer that does not exist."
        next
      end

      pats = Patterns.new(froms.map { |f| File.join(dir, f) },
                          keys_from.map { |f| File.join(dir, f) })
      block.body.each_with_index do |line, k|
        next if line.strip.empty?

        lineno = block.fence_line + 1 + k
        next if pats.accounts_for?(line)

        if line.strip.start_with?("{") && line.strip.end_with?("}")
          begin
            obj = JSON.parse(line.strip)
          rescue JSON::ParserError
            obj = nil
          end
          if obj.is_a?(Hash)
            unknown_keys = []
            walk = lambda do |node|
              case node
              when Hash then node.each { |kk, vv| unknown_keys << kk unless pats.key?(kk); walk.call(vv) }
              when Array then node.each { |vv| walk.call(vv) }
              end
            end
            walk.call(obj)
            if unknown_keys.empty?
              counts[:json] += 1
              next
            end
            errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: the JSON summary line carries key(s) " \
                      "#{unknown_keys.uniq.join(', ')} that no file in `from:` or " \
                      "`keys_from:` names. A published summary naming a field the driver stopped " \
                      "emitting is K-1053 exactly; a nested key belongs to the handler that " \
                      "serialises it, so name that handler in `keys_from:`."
            next
          end
        end

        errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: this line is not a line `#{task}` prints — no string " \
                  "literal in #{froms.join(', ')} produces it:\n      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
      end
      counts[:transcript] += 1
      puts "  transcript #{where}  #{task}  <- #{froms.join(' ')}" if listing
    else
      src_path = File.join(dir, froms.first)
      if froms.length > 1
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: snippet` takes exactly one `from:` — a snippet quotes ONE " \
                  "file, and a union of files is not a thing a reader can go and check."
        next
      end

      transforms = block.decl["transform"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject { |t| t.empty? || t == "none" }
      bad = transforms - TRANSFORMS
      unless bad.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: transform(s) #{bad.join(', ')} are not implemented here. The declared " \
                  "transformation has to be EXECUTABLE; #{TRANSFORMS.join(', ')} are."
        next
      end

      if abridged == "none" && !transforms.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `abridged: none` beside `transform: #{transforms.join(', ')}`. A " \
                  "transformation IS an abridgement — say what it takes out."
      end

      src = File.read(src_path)
      raw_lines = Set.new(src.split("\n", -1).map(&:rstrip))
      allowed = raw_lines.dup
      transforms.each { |t| transformed_lines(src, t).each { |l| allowed << l.rstrip } }
      lstripped = Set.new(allowed.map(&:lstrip)) if transforms.include?("dedent")
      needed_transform = false

      # The first line may be the conventional `# <path>` banner. It is prose,
      # not a line of the file — but it must name the file the declaration does.
      body = block.body
      if (m = body.first.to_s.match(%r{\A#\s*([A-Za-z0-9_./-]+)}))
        if m[1] == froms.first
          body = body[1..]
        else
          errors << "#{where}: the block's opening banner names #{m[1]} while the declaration says " \
                    "`from: #{froms.first}`. One of the two is wrong and a reader cannot tell which."
          body = body[1..]
        end
      end

      in_note = false
      body.each_with_index do |line, k|
        lineno = block.fence_line + (block.body.length - body.length) + 1 + k
        if line =~ ELISION_MARKER
          if abridged == "none"
            errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: a marked elision inside a block declared " \
                      "`abridged: none`. The declaration and the block disagree."
          end
          in_note = true
          counts[:elision] += 1
          next
        end
        if in_note
          next if line.strip.start_with?("#")

          in_note = false
        end
        next if line.strip.empty?
        if allowed.include?(line.rstrip) || lstripped&.include?(line.lstrip.rstrip)
          needed_transform ||= !raw_lines.include?(line.rstrip)
          next
        end

        if line.strip.start_with?("#") && !src.include?(line.strip)
          errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: a comment line that is not in #{froms.first} and is not " \
                    "introduced by the marked-elision line. A document comment a reader cannot " \
                    "tell from shipped code is the whole reason that marker exists:\n" \
                    "      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
          next
        end

        errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: this line is in no version of #{froms.first} " \
                  "(raw#{transforms.empty? ? '' : " or #{transforms.join('/')}"}):\n" \
                  "      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
      end
      if !transforms.empty? && !needed_transform
        errors << "#{where}: `transform: #{transforms.join(', ')}` is declared but no line in the " \
                  "block needs it — every quoted line is already in the raw file. A transformation " \
                  "is declared because it was APPLIED; a decorative one is a prose promise again."
      end
      counts[:snippet] += 1
      puts "  snippet    #{where}  #{froms.first}#{transforms.empty? ? '' : "  [#{transforms.join(' ')}]"}" if listing
    end
  end
end

if counts[:transcript].zero? && counts[:snippet].zero? && errors.empty?
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: found NO declared blocks at all. A rule that inspects nothing " \
            "passes everything."
end

if errors.empty?
  puts "check-demo-derivations: OK — #{counts[:transcript]} transcript and #{counts[:snippet]} " \
       "snippet blocks across #{(DOCUMENTED_DEMOS & present).size} narrative documents, every line " \
       "held to a literal its declared producer prints or a line of the file it quotes " \
       "(#{counts[:json]} JSON summary line(s) key-checked, #{counts[:elision]} marked elision(s), " \
       "#{counts[:none]} block(s) declared underived with a reason)."
  exit 0
end

warn "\ncheck-demo-derivations FAILED:\n"
errors.each { |e| warn "  ✗ #{e}" }
warn "\nEvery fenced block in a kiosk-demo-*/before-after.md must declare its producer in an " \
     "`<!-- derived: … -->` comment, and every line in it must be a line that producer prints or " \
     "a line of the file it quotes. Abridgement is DECLARED, never promised in prose."
exit 1
